lesson_number: 6
title: Tools
topic_summary: 'Introduce tools/function calling as the mechanism allowing LLMs to overcome static knowledge cutoffs by interacting
  with external environments. Cover the fundamental request-execute-respond loop, tool schema design, and why clear descriptions/names
  are critical for reliable selection. Progress from implementing tool calling and a decorator-based registry from scratch,
  to leveraging production SDKs like Gemini for native function calling, combining with Pydantic for validated structured
  outputs, implementing multi-step loops, and analyzing limitations that motivate advanced patterns.

  '
why_valuable: 'Tool calling is a foundational skill for AI engineers building production agents that must query databases,
  call APIs, execute code, or access memory. Deep understanding of schema design, parsing, loops, and SDK patterns enables
  debugging unreliable agents, reducing custom prompt fragility, and scaling to complex multi-tool workflows.

  '
target_length_words: 3600
theory_practice_ratio: 40% theory - 60% practice
lesson_scope: 'Lesson 6 builds directly on prior coverage of context engineering, structured outputs (Lesson 4), and workflow
  patterns like chaining/routing; it sets up ReAct-style reasoning loops (Lessons 7-8), memory systems (Lesson 9), and RAG/agentic
  RAG (Lesson 10).

  '
audience: 'AI engineers who have completed earlier lessons on LLM fundamentals, prompting, structured outputs with Pydantic,
  and basic orchestration patterns, and who now want to give models the ability to act on external systems.

  '
concepts_from_previous_lessons:
- Structured outputs via Pydantic models and JSON schema generation (Lesson 4)
- Chaining and routing as basic LLM workflow patterns
- Context engineering and system prompt design for controlling model behavior
concepts_for_future_lessons:
- ReAct (Reason + Act) interleaving of explicit reasoning steps with tool calls (Lessons 7-8)
- Memory architectures including vector and graph databases (Lesson 9)
- RAG and agentic RAG patterns that rely on memory-access tools (Lesson 10)
article_code:
- title: Notebook 1
  url: https://github.com/towardsai/course-ai-agents/blob/dev/lessons/06_tools/notebook.ipynb
sections:
- title: Introduction
  target_words: 135
  key_points:
  - Callback to core concepts from previous lessons as the entry point
  - 'Transition to this lesson: tools as the mechanism that lets LLMs act beyond text
    generation'
- title: Understanding Why Agents Need Tools
  target_words: 310
  key_points:
  - 'Core LLM limitation: static training cutoff prevents real-time data access or
    external actions'
  - 'Brain-and-hands analogy: LLM is the reasoning brain; tools are the hands and
    senses for perceiving and acting in the world'
  - 'Common production tool categories: API calls, database queries, memory/RAG retrieval,
    sandboxed code execution'
  - 'Diagram: tool output becomes feedback that the LLM interprets to decide its next
    action or produce a final answer'
- title: Implementing Tool Calls from Scratch
  target_words: 930
  key_points:
  - 'Request-execute-respond loop from scratch: expose tools via system prompt, parse
    LLM''s structured function call, execute handler, inject result, generate final
    response'
  - 'Tool schema anatomy: name, description, JSON Schema for parameters (types, required
    fields, per-param descriptions)'
  - 'Tool registry pattern: map from name to handler + schema to avoid duplication
    and enable dynamic dispatch'
  - 'System prompt engineering: describe when to call tools, enforce output format,
    and emphasise description clarity to prevent selection errors'
  - 'Response parsing and execution: extract structured call, dispatch to handler,
    return result -- handle malformed calls gracefully'
  - 'Tradeoff: manual schemas give full control but violate DRY and become a maintenance
    burden as tool count grows'
- title: Building a Decorator-Based Tool Framework
  target_words: 560
  key_points:
  - 'Motivation for the @tool decorator: automate schema generation from function
    signature, type hints, and docstring to enforce DRY'
  - 'Custom tool wrapper class: bundle handler function and auto-generated JSON Schema
    into one modular unit'
  - 'Decorator implementation: derive parameter schema from type hints and docstring
    automatically, removing manual schema duplication'
  - Registry as a list of tool objects; derived maps for by-name lookup and schema
    list for prompting
  - 'Comparison to production frameworks like LangGraph: same principle of centralising
    schema logic for scalability'
  - Python decorator mechanics as function composition
- title: Production Tool Calling with the Gemini SDK
  target_words: 390
  key_points:
  - 'Native SDK advantage over manual prompts: pass tool definitions directly as SDK
    objects; no custom parsing needed'
  - 'SDK execution pattern: configure tool mode, receive structured function call
    response, dispatch to handler, feed typed result back'
  - 'SDK auto-schema generation: pass Python functions directly; provider extracts
    signature and docstring automatically'
  - 'Portability insight: equivalent patterns exist across major providers; skills
    transfer with minimal changes'
- title: Using Pydantic Models as Tools for On-Demand Structured Outputs
  target_words: 315
  key_points:
  - 'Hybrid pattern: agent reasons in natural language internally, then calls a Pydantic
    model as a tool only for final validated output'
  - 'Treat a Pydantic model as a tool: derive its JSON Schema for the tool definition,
    invoke only at the output extraction step'
  - 'Validation benefit: Pydantic raises errors on schema or type mismatch before
    data reaches downstream systems'
  - 'Contrast with always-structured mode: retaining free-form reasoning until final
    extraction gives the agent more flexibility'
- title: The Downsides of Running Tools in a Loop
  target_words: 520
  key_points:
  - 'Iterative loop: allow the LLM to chain multiple tool calls, deciding what tool
    to use at each step based on previous outputs'
  - 'Loop message-structure diagram: user prompt -- tool call -- tool result -- tool
    call -- ... -- final answer'
  - 'Core limitations of sequential loops: no reasoning between calls, limited planning,
    risk of inefficient usage or infinite loops'
  - 'Parallel tool execution: independent tools can run simultaneously to reduce latency
    when order does not matter'
  - 'Bridge to ReAct: these loop limitations motivate explicit reasoning steps before
    each tool decision -- preview of Lessons 7-8'
- title: Popular Tools Used Within the Industry
  target_words: 340
  key_points:
  - 'Knowledge and memory access tools: vector DB queries, text-to-SQL, graph DB
    retrieval -- closely related to memory and RAG lessons'
  - 'Web search and browsing tools: search engine API integrations and web scrapers
    -- common in chatbots and research agents'
  - 'Code execution tools: sandboxed interpreter for calculations, data manipulation,
    and visualisation'
  - 'Other enterprise categories: external API integrations (calendar, email, project
    management), file system operations'
- title: Conclusion
  target_words: 90
  key_points:
  - Tool calling as the foundational primitive for any production agent that must
    act beyond text generation
  - Forward link to Lessons 7-8 on ReAct reasoning patterns that overcome the limitations
    of naive tool loops
